## Memory & Ledger Discipline

The memory stack is self-injecting and self-monitoring. There is no bot to
call.

- **MEMORY.md is auto-injected** at SessionStart (the `## ⏯ LATEST` resume
  anchor + the curated claude-mem render). Its `## INVARIANTS` section
  links the first-class invariant files (`invariant_*.md` in the memory
  dir). **The invariants ritual: actively re-read them at every arc
  boundary and before any design pass** — passive injection gets skimmed.
  The invariants carry the load-bearing "why" that machinery cannot
  recover if it decays.
- **One append-only ledger per arc.** A single markdown file
  (`.superpowers/sdd/progress.md` pattern) the arc appends to at every
  decision, gate verdict, or halt. Append; never rewrite history. On
  resume, read the ledger tail FIRST so you do not re-dispatch completed
  work.
- **History questions → claude-mem CLI.** For "have we done X / what did we
  decide about Y," query the hybrid (BM25 + vector) index rather than
  re-deriving: `claude-mem search "your question"`.
- **The memory-health gate line appears at SessionStart** (`MEMORY-HEALTH:
  N/10 GREEN`). GREEN = the stack is live. **RED means
  fix-before-proceeding** — a red check names the broken store and its fix
  hint; silent memory-system death is the failure mode this gate exists to
  prevent.
- **Memory file naming convention** (one file per durable fact, ONE-line
  index entry in MEMORY.md): `invariant_*.md` (first-class, re-read at arc
  boundaries) · `feedback_*.md` (hard rules / corrections from the
  operator) · `project_*.md` (load-bearing architectural facts and plans) ·
  `reference_*.md` (how to use a tool) · `checkpoint_YYYY_MM_DD_*.md`
  (resume anchors) · `process_*.md` (operational how-to knowledge).

